# Suppressing the metal-metal interaction by CoZn0.5V1.5O4 derived from two-dimensional metal-organic frameworks for supercapacitors

Suppressing the metal-metal interaction by $\mathsf{C o Z n}_{0.5}\mathsf{V}_{1.5}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ derived from two-dimensional metal-organic frameworks for supercapacitors
Keywords: supercapacitors, metal-metal interaction, octahedral, tetrahedral, metal-organic frameworks
## INTRODUCTION
To mitigate energy crisis, it is important to develop high-efficiency energy storage devices with high energy density, high power density, and long cycling life [1–3]. Supercapacitors, emerging as energy storage devices, have received increasing attention in electric vehicles, intermittent wind, and solar energy systems owing to their short charge-discharge time, high power density, long cycle life, and high safety [4–8]. The electrode materials of supercapacitors can be divided into carbon materials, conductive polymers, and transition metal oxides according to different charge storage mechanisms [9]. Electric doublelayered capacitive carbon materials that store charges with fast ion adsorption and desorption on the electrode surface can achieve high power density and long life; however, their theoretical specific capacitance is low [10,11]. Pseudocapacitive conductive polymers, N-doped carbon materials, and transition metal oxides provide higher theoretical specific capacitance owing to their fast redox reactions at the electrode/electrolyte interface [12–14]. However, conductive polymers usually exhibit poor durability, which limits their wide range of applications [15]. For N-doped carbon materials, lone-pair electrons of N can enhance the pseudocapacitance storage, but the energy density per unit mass is still limited [16–19]. Transition metal oxides, such as Co and V, which exhibit higher theoretical capacitance owing to their high oxidation state, have been considered as promising electrode materials for supercapacitors. After decades of development, research on both the capacity and stability of transition metal oxides for supercapacitors has achieved great progress [13]; however, the areal energy density remains a challenge for future applications. To increase the number of active sites per unit area, nanostructures with super-high specific area have constantly been chosen, which decrease the stability during charging and discharging cycles [20].
Moreover, the specific capacitance of supercapacitors is closely related to the specific surface area of the electrode materials [27–29]. MOFs have unique physicochemical properties owing to their specific morphologies and high specific surface areas [29–35]. They reveal many advantages in gas separation, fluorescence sensing, catalysis, electrochemistry, and other fields [36–38]. For example, Bai et al. [36] reported $\mathrm{{Ni}/\mathrm{{Co}}}$ bimetallic MOF nanoplates, which exhibited excellent oxygen evolution reaction electrocatalytic activity with a low overpotential of $256\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ and a small Tafel slope of $8\bar{1}.8\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ in KOH with long-term electrochemical stability for 3000 cyclic voltammetry (CV) cycles. The recrystallization process of MOFs can maintain their original morphology and introduce doping atoms for functional applications, such as porous carbon [39,40], metal oxides [41,42], and sulfides [43,44]. Guan et al. [41] reported hollow and porous $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanostructures fabricated using two-dimensional solid nanowall arrays of Co-MOF through an ion-exchange and etching process with an additional annealing treatment. The as-fabricated $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanowall electrode shows remarkable capacitance $(1055.3\mathrm{Fg}^{-1}$ at $2.5\operatorname{mA}{\mathrm{cm}^{-2}}$ ). Young et al. [42] converted the bimetallic NiCoMOF-74 particles into graphitic carbon $/\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}$ composites and $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}/\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}$ composites, which exhibited high specific capacitances of 715 and $513\mathrm{Fg}^{-1}$ , respectively. Zhang et al. [45] utilized porous graphitic carbon, which was co-doped with $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ and S, to encapsulate $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ MOFs $(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}/\mathrm{NS-C})$ to enhance the stability of the electrode (capacitance retention of $99.8\%$ after 140,000 cycles).
In this study, we propose a simple method to synthesize $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ through the ion-exchange and recrystallization of MOFs of zinc-cobalt (ZnCo-MOF) and sodium metavanadate $(\mathrm{NaVO}_{3})$ . As a late transition metal, Zn assumes the place of the Co octahedron, which was identified by Raman and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). According to theoretical calculations, the $\mathrm{Co(O_{6})\mathrm{-V}}$ interaction repels the 3d orbital near the Fermi level and acts as a semiconductor. The doping of $Z\mathrm{n}$ can decrease the metal-metal effect and result in exits of the degenerate state at the Fermi level. The synthesized samples of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ exhibit high specific capacitance ( $\mathrm{1100}\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at a current density of $1\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ ), enhanced rate capability $(81\%$ specific capacitance retention after 10 times increase in the current density), and long cycling durability $(99.4\%$ capacitance retention after 18,000 cycles).
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
## Fabrication of ZnCo-MOF
Approximately 1 mmol zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and 2 mmol cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ were dissolved in $50\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water and stirred at room temperature for $30\mathrm{min}$ to form solution A. Thereafter, 16 mmol 2-methylimidazole (2-MIM) was dissolved in another $50\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water and stirred for $30\mathrm{min}$ to obtain solution B. Solution A was added quickly to solution B, and then stirred vigorously for 1 min. Subsequently, a piece of cleaned nickel foam (NF) $(2\mathsf{c m}\times4\mathsf{c m})$ was washed with
$0.5\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ hydrochloric acid, ethanol, and water in turn and then immersed into the above mixture for ZnCo-MOF precursor growth. After $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ , the NF turned purple and was removed from the mixture and washed several times with deionized water and ethanol. Thereafter, it was vacuum-dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ .
## Synthesis of $\mathbf{CoZn_{0.5}V_{1.5}O_{4}}$
A piece of the as-fabricated NF with ${\mathrm{ZnCo-MOF}}$ was immersed in a solution of $4\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\mathrm{NaVO}_{3}$ After $2\mathrm{h}$ of reaction at room temperature, it was removed, cleaned with deionized water, and then dried in a vacuum oven at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ . Thereafter, it was heated in a muffle furnace at $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ to obtain the $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ sample, which was grown on NF $\mathrm{(CoZn_{0.5}V_{1.5}O_{4}/}$ NF). The mass loading of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was $\sim2.11\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .
## Synthesis of $\mathbf{Co_{2}V O_{4}}$
Solution A was synthesized using 2 mmol of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ dissolved in $50\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water. The other steps were the same as those used for the fabrication of ${\mathrm{ZnCo-MOF}}$ and $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ to acquire Co-MOF and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4},$ respectively. Similarly, we obtained a sample of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ grown on NF $\left({\mathrm{Co}}_{2}{\mathrm{VO}}_{4}/{\mathrm{NF}}\right)$ . The mass loading of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ was $\widetilde{\sim}2.04\mathrm{mgcm}^{-2}$ .
## Material characterization
The morphologies and structures of the samples were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, S-4800), transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEOL-2100), and highresolution TEM (HRTEM, field emission JEOL-2100). The crystalline structure of the material was investigated by X-ray diffraction (XRD, RigataD/max-C diffractometer with a $\mathrm{{Cu-Ka}}$ radiation source, $\lambda=1.5406\mathring\mathrm{A},$ ) over the $2\theta$ range of $10^{\circ}$ to $80^{\circ}$ . The elements on the surface were analyzed by XPS (RBD upgraded PHI-5000C ESCA system with $\operatorname{Mg-Ka}$ radiation, $h\nu=$ $1253.6\mathrm{eV})$ . Raman spectra were obtained on a Raman spectrometer (Jobin-Yvon HR800) from 200 to $1600{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ using a $514\mathrm{nm}$ argon ion laser. Nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms and Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) pore diameter distribution curves were characterized using a Quantchome Autosorb-1 analyzer.
## Electrochemical measurements
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{C=\displaystyle\frac{I\Delta t}{S\Delta V},}}\\ {{E=\displaystyle\frac{1}{7200}C(\Delta V)^{2},}}\\ {{P=3600\displaystyle\frac{E}{\Delta V},}}\end{array}
$$
where $I$ is the constant discharge current (mA), $\Delta t$ is the discharge time (s), S is the effective area of the active substance of the work electrode $(\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ , and $\Delta V$ is the discharge voltage range (V) [46].
## Density functional theory (DFT) calculations
The structural changes induced by $Z\mathrm{n}$ doping were studied using DFT. Our calculations were performed using the Vienna ab initio simulation package (VASP) [47], and the exchange and correlation energy functional was treated by the Perdew-BurkeErnzerh variant of the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) [48]. Interactions between ions and electrons were described using the projector augmented wave pseudo potentials (PAW) approach, and the energy cutoff for the plane wave basis set was set to $500\mathrm{eV}$ ; the total energy was converged to $10^{-5}\mathrm{eV}$ [49,50]. Effective $U$ parameters of 5.7 and $3.1\mathrm{eV}$ were used to describe the electronic structures of Co and V, respectively [51]. The lattice parameters of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ were optimized to be $6.00\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times6.00\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times6.06\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ with $\alpha=120^{\circ}$ , $\beta=120^{\circ}$ , γ $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 89^{\circ}$ and $6.00\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times6.05\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times6.00\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ with $\alpha~=~\beta~=~\gamma~=~60^{\circ},$ respectively. Seven $k$ -points were used for $k$ -point sampling in the irreducible region of the first Brillouin zone, which ensured the accuracy of the calculation within $1\mathrm{meV}$ .
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The synthesis process of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is shown in Fig. S1. Spinel $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ was synthesized by the exchange of $2{\cdot}\mathrm{MIM}^{-}$ in Co-MOF by ${\mathrm{VO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , where one $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ assumes the tetrahedral site, and one Co and one $\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ occupy the octahedral sites, as shown in Fig. 1a, b. To replace octahedral cobalt with zinc, a precursor of ZnCo-MOF $\left(\mathrm{Co}{:}\mathrm{Zn}~=~2{:}1\right) $ ) was selected for the synthesis of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (Fig. 1d), and the Co octahedrons were occupied by $Z\mathrm{n}$ and V.
The crystalline structures of the materials were investigated using XRD. From the XRD spectra (Fig. 2a), the patterns of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ are consistent with the standard pattern of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ (PDF card No. 73-1633) without any peaks corresponding to ZnCo-MOF or Co-MOF (Fig. S2) [52,53], suggesting the formation of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}.$ respectively.
In the Raman spectra of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ (Fig. 2b), the peak at $807\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponds to $_{\mathrm{V-O}}$ bonds [54], and the peaks at 314 and $477\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to the tetrahedral ${\mathrm{Co-O}}$ bonds [52,55,56]. Moreover, the peak at $678\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which is attributed to the octahedral $\mathrm{Co-O}$ bonds of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ , almost disappeared in $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ and no peaks corresponding to ZnCo-MOF (Fig. S2) were observed, which suggests that the octahedral Co atoms were replaced by $Z\mathrm{n}$ and V atoms [55,56].
XPS was used to examine the surface states of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ . The full spectra (Fig. 2c) show Co, V, and O elements in both $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ . The extra peak of Zn $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ at $1021\mathrm{eV}$ in $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ indicates the presence of Zn. In $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}.$ the V $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ peak (Fig. 2d) corresponds to $\mathrm{V}^{4+}$ $(516.7\mathrm{eV})$ . The V $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ peak of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (Fig. 2e) can be deconvoluted into $\mathrm{V}^{4+}$ $\cdot\ (516.7\mathrm{eV})$ and $\mathrm{V}^{3+}$ $^{3+}\:(515.5\mathrm{eV})$ [54]. This is because $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ reduced $\mathrm{V}^{4+}$ to $\mathrm{V}^{3+}$ . The O 1s spectrum (Fig. 2f) of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ demonstrates the coexistence of metal–oxygen bonds $(529.6\mathrm{eV})$ , hypoxia defects $(531.3\mathrm{eV})$ , and adsorbed water $(531.8\mathrm{eV})$ on the surface [41,57].
SEM images (Fig. S3a, c) show that the ZnCo-MOF and CoMOF nanosheet arrays grow uniformly and are closely arranged on the surface of the NF. From the enlarged views (Fig. S3b, d), the surfaces of the ZnCo-MOF and Co-MOF nanosheets were smooth and similar in size. The nanosheet array structure of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (Fig. 3a) can still be well maintained after treatment with the $\mathrm{NaVO}_{3}$ solution. The SEM image of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (Fig. 3b) reveals that the surface becomes rough, and small particles can be detected. Comparing the $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (Fig. 3c) and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ nanosheets (Fig. 3d), the thickness of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ is approximately $250\mathrm{nm}$ , while that of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is approximately $180\mathrm{nm}$ . The nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms (Fig. S2c) and BJH pore size distributions (Fig. S2d) show the specific surface area and mesoporous features. The surface areas of the two electrodes were measured using the BrunauerEmmett-Teller (BET) method. On the NF, the specific surface area of the $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $(11.2\mathsf{c m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ is larger than that of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ $\mathrm{10.4cm^{2}g^{-1}})$ . Thus, doping with Zn does not decrease the specific surface area. The specific surface areas of Co-MOF and ${\mathrm{ZnCo-MOF}}$ are 9.12 and $9.60\mathsf{c m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, as shown in Fig. S4. The specific surface area of ZnCo-MOF was slightly larger than that of Co-MOF. The specific surface areas of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ after ion exchange and annealing treatment were improved compared with those of ${\mathrm{ZnCo-MOF}}$ and Co-MOF.
Fig. 3e, f show TEM and HRTEM images of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4},$ respectively. The inset TEM images show that the nanosheets are composed of many small dense particles. The elemental distribution was further confirmed by energy-dispersive X-ray spectrometry (EDS) mapping analysis (Fig. 3g), where Zn, Co, V, and O elements are uniformly distributed on the $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanosheets. The HRTEM images show obvious lattice fringes of $2.09\mathring\mathrm{A}$ in the ${\mathrm{Co}}_{2}{\mathrm{VO}}_{4},$ which matches well with the standard (400) crystal planes of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ [21]. An expanded $d$ spacing of $2.13\mathring\mathrm{A}$ is observed in $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ for the (400) facets, which is consistent with the XRD results. Further, hexagonal symmetry can be detected, which agrees well with the analyses of Sekhar [20].
DFT calculations were performed to study the changes in the electronic structure of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ by $Z\mathrm{n}$ doping. The structure of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ is shown in Fig. 1b, where one ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ cation occupies the tetrahedral site and the other ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{V}^{4+}$ occupy the octahedral sites. All the octahedrons of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{O}_{6})$ and $\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{O}_{6})$ are connected via edge-sharing bonds, and the $\mathrm{Co(O_{4})}$ tetrahedrons link with the octahedrons by corner-sharing bonds. Although the surface of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ is hexagonal, its high Co content makes it exhibit a layered structure [21]. The structure of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is shown in Fig. 1d, where half of the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ octahedrons in $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ are replaced by $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and the other half are replaced by $\mathrm{V}^{3+}$ , thus reducing the two $\mathrm{V}^{4+}$ ions in $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ to $\mathrm{V}^{3+}$ . The new structure exhibited a high hexagonal symmetry.
To understand the electronic properties of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ DOS analysis was performed, as shown in Fig. 4a, b. $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ behaves as a semiconductor in addition to the existence of $\mathrm{V^{4+}}$ , which is similar to $\mathrm{VO}_{2}$ . This phenomenon has been proposed as a crystalline field splitting of the d orbitals, which is caused by strong electron-electron interactions in metal-metal bonds [58]. Further, projected DOS (PDOS) calculations were used to explain this splitting effect, as shown in
The electrochemical performance of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was measured in a three-electrode system with $2{\mathrm{mol}}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ KOH aqueous solution as the electrolyte. NF-loaded $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was directly applied as the working electrode without extra binder or conductive carbon materials. The $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ was tested under the same conditions for comparison.
The CV curves of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ at a scanning rate of $5\mathrm{mVs}^{-1}$ are shown in Fig. 5a. $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ presents a larger CV curve area than ${\mathrm{Co}}_{2}{\mathrm{VO}}_{4},$ indicating an enhanced specific capacitance for $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . The CV curves of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ at different scanning rates are shown in Fig. S5a. A pair of redox peaks belonging to $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ [54,61] within the potential range of 0 to $0.45\mathrm{V}$ in all the CV curves indicates pseudocapacitance energy storage characteristics when used as electrodes for supercapacitors [62]. As the scanning rate increased, the retention rates of the $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ CV curves were better than those of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}.$ , showing a good capacitive output capability at high scanning rates. Fig. S5b shows the GCD curves of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ at different current densities. The curves exhibit nonlinear and approximately symmetric graphical features. They have a pair of charge and discharge level platforms, in agreement with the redox peaks in the CV curves. The GCD curves of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ at $1\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ (Fig. 5b) illustrate that $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ has a longer discharge time than $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4},$ indicating that $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ has a larger specific capacitance. In addition, the GCD curve of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ has poor symmetry, and the calculated coulombic efficiency is only $82\%$ , while that of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is as high as $99\%$ . As the current density increased, the coulombic efficiency of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ approaches $100\%$ . The capacitance values of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ (Fig. 5c) can be calculated using the GCD curve. The capacitances of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ are 1100, 1050, 966, 949, and $890\mathrm{mFcm}^{-2}$ at current densities of 1, 3, 5, 7, and $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, which are higher than those of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ (915, 880, 845, 710, $599\mathrm{mFcm}^{-2}$ ). When the current density was increased from 1 to $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , the capacitance of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was $81\%$ . $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ delivered a capacitance of $915\mathrm{mFcm}^{-2}$ at $1\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and only $65\%$ remained when the current density was increased to $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ . In Fig. 5d, when the power density is $0.225\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , the energy density of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is $0.031\mathrm{mWh}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is higher than that of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ 1 $\left<0.026\mathrm{mWh}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\right>$ and other reported multi-metal compounds [20,52,63]. The above studies show that replacement with zinc can effectively improve the coulombic efficiency, capacitance content, and rate performance of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ .
The kinetic factors for the enhanced electrochemical performance were analyzed using alternating current (AC) impedance spectroscopy. EIS curves (Fig. 5e) consist of steep straight lines in the low-frequency region (related to charge diffusion) and semicircles in the high-frequency region (related to charge transfer) [46]. From the EIS, the electrolyte resistances $(R_{s})$ of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ are similar. The electron transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $\left(0.18\Omega\right)$ is smaller than that of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ $\left(0.4\Omega\right)$ , indicating fast electron transfer ability. Meanwhile, the slope of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ in the low-frequency region is larger than that of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4};$ thus, its ion diffusion kinetics is enhanced by the addition of $Z\mathrm{n}$ [66]. Hence, the doping of $Z\mathrm{n}$ in $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ increases the electron transfer and ion diffusion speed, which is in good agreement with the calculation results.
The cycling performances of the $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ electrodes were tested at a current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ . As shown in Fig. 7a, the specific capacitance of the $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ electrode retained $99.4\%$ of its initial capacitance after 18,000 charge/discharge cycles, and the retention of the $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ electrode was only $87\%$ , indicating improved cycling durability of $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . In the SEM after 18,000 cycles (Fig. 7e), the structure of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ changed from nanosheets to nanoparticles owing to the large resistance of charge diffusion during the electrochemical reaction. The morphology of the $\mathrm{CoZn}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{1.5}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanosheets (Fig. 7d) is retained, indicating that the introduction of $Z\mathrm{n}$ ions can improve the structural stability of the $Z\mathrm{n-O}$ covalent bond.
